{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>ופתחו [its gates].</b> Some say the hirik is in place of a shureq. Some say that ופתחו refers back to בני נכר, and this is not far-fetched. The meaning is known: to bring to you the wealth of nations by day and also by night." "<b>ומלכיהם [and their kings].</b> That they were led — יובאו נהוגים — as in the sense of 'to bind their kings with chains' (Psalms 149:8)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 60, :notes {0 "The referent of ופתחו and its connection to the preceding phrase 'בני נכר' (v. 10) is implied; rendered literally without expansion.", 1 "The phrase 'שהיו נוהגים יובאו נהוגים' plays on the root נ-ה-ג; rendered literally to preserve the wordplay."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:00:30.027804868Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}